# 3. HIGH-FIDELITY GEOMETRY: UPGRADE THE ACTUAL ANATOMICAL MODEL

This is one of the highest-priority tasks.

The existing application uses BodyParts3D 4.0 meshes. Preserve that dataset as a useful baseline, but do not treat it as the final quality ceiling.

We need more detailed actual anatomy, not just visual shaders applied to existing simplified meshes.

## 3.1 Preserve original geometry

Inspect:

- Original source mesh resolution.
- Existing optimized mesh resolution.
- Per-mesh triangle counts.
- Vertex counts.
- Normal precision.
- Mesh boundaries.
- Small structures and thin anatomical elements.
- Anatomical details that may have been lost in optimization.

The existing `scripts/optimize-anatomy.mjs` requests a reduced triangle target of approximately 22% of the original index count, subject to its simplification error tolerance.

Do not blindly use this same reduction strategy for every anatomical structure.

A large superficial muscle and a small cranial nerve must not receive identical optimization treatment.

Implement a quality-aware geometry pipeline.

Requirements:

- Preserve source geometry before optimization.
- Preserve high-resolution source variants.
- Never destructively replace the highest-quality source assets.
- Preserve source-to-output provenance.
- Record original and optimized triangle counts per structure.
- Record optimization method and measured error.
- Create separate high-, medium-, and low-detail versions.
- Apply optimization according to structure-specific requirements.
- Preserve thin vessels, small nerves, small foramina, delicate structures, and anatomical landmarks.
- Avoid destroying clinically relevant surface detail.
- Preserve or correctly regenerate vertex normals.
- Preserve sharp edges where anatomically appropriate.
- Avoid smoothing distinct anatomical boundaries into one surface.
- Validate mesh bounds and index integrity.
- Detect NaN and infinite geometry values.
- Detect degenerate and zero-area triangles where relevant.
- Detect duplicate geometry and unintended overlapping surfaces.

Do not confuse a reduction in file size with an improvement in anatomical quality.

## 3.2 Introduce a structure-specific geometry quality policy

Every anatomical mesh should be able to define:

- Minimum acceptable triangle count.
- Source resolution.
- Maximum allowed simplification error.
- Required preservation level.
- Whether simplification is permitted.
- Whether the mesh requires sharp-edge preservation.
- Whether the mesh requires high-resolution detail near camera.
- Whether the mesh is a critical anatomical landmark.
- Whether the structure is too small or delicate for aggressive optimization.

Examples:

- Cranial nerves: very high preservation priority.
- Major vessels: high preservation priority.
- Small arteries: high preservation priority.
- Articular surfaces: high preservation priority.
- Ligaments: high preservation priority.
- Tendons: high preservation priority.
- Facial structures: high preservation priority.
- Fine muscle attachments: high preservation priority.
- Large non-critical surfaces: adaptive optimization may be allowed.

Do not invent quality measurements. Measure them from the actual assets.

## 3.3 Progressive geometry and Level of Detail

Implement a scalable Level of Detail architecture.

Provide:

- LOD 0: original or highest-quality available geometry.
- LOD 1: high-quality optimized geometry.
- LOD 2: medium-detail geometry.
- LOD 3: lightweight geometry for mobile or distant viewing.

LOD switching should consider screen-space size, viewing distance, hardware capabilities, and user-selected quality settings.

The system must dynamically load detailed anatomy when the user zooms into a structure and release unnecessary geometry when appropriate.

No visual popping, disappearing structures, broken selection, or mismatched anatomical positions during LOD transitions.

For missing high-resolution source geometry, retain the best verified existing mesh and clearly report the limitation.

